==============================================================================================================================
ITEM 109 -- the halo spin parameter predicted from the baryons
==============================================================================================================================
  Li+2020 J/ApJS/247/31: 12 halo models x 175 SPARC galaxies
  convention recovered from the table itself: V200/(C200 rs) = 0.7300 km/s/kpc = 10 H0 with H0 = 73.0 km/s/Mpc
  [PASS] 109-setup Li+2020's R200 = C200 x r_s and V200 = 10 H0 R200 with H0 = 73, recovered from their own table to 0.5%; the same H0 is used on BOTH sides here so the comparison is internally consistent   (implied H0 = 73.00 km/s/Mpc, spread 0.4%)
  j measured for 137/147 SPARC discs (cuts: >=4 photometric points, r_max/R_d >= 2, tail < 50% of j)
  the exponential tail beyond the last measured point carries a median 6% of j (90th pct 36%) -- it is NOT negligible and it is the largest modelling choice in j
  [PASS] 109-j the measured j reproduces the analytic 2 R_d V_flat of an ideal exponential disc with a flat curve to better than 0.1 dex in the median -- the estimator is not broken   (median log10(j_measured / 2 R_d V_flat) = -0.038 dex, scatter 0.114, N = 116)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
the framework's V200 and lambda (zero parameters: photometry, the rotation curve, and a_0)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  canonical : V200 median   98.7 km/s, R200 median  135.2 kpc, y = g_bar/a_0 at R200 median 6.07e-04 (deep MOND, so V200 = (G M_b a_0)^1/4 to 0.62%)
              lambda: median 0.0247, 16-84% [0.0139, 0.0356], width 0.220 dex
  alt       : V200 median  103.4 km/s, R200 median  141.6 kpc, y = g_bar/a_0 at R200 median 4.58e-04 (deep MOND, so V200 = (G M_b a_0)^1/4 to 0.54%)
              lambda: median 0.0225, 16-84% [0.0127, 0.0326], width 0.220 dex
  [PASS] 109f the framework's sharpest statement here, tested on its own: deep MOND forces V200 = V_flat EXACTLY, and SPARC's measured V_flat reproduces the predicted V200 to within 0.05 dex in the median with only 0.06 dex of scatter.  This is the BTFR wearing a virial-velocity label, and it must be quoted that way and not as an independent success   (median log10(V200_pred/V_flat) = -0.050 dex (canonical) / -0.030 (alt), scatter 0.059 dex over N = 116)
  the M/L-FREE form: because V200 = V_flat identically, lambda = 10 H0 j/(sqrt(2) V_flat^2) can be evaluated with NO stellar mass-to-light ratio anywhere -- j is Upsilon-free by construction and V_flat is measured.  Median 0.0190, width 0.226 dex, on N = 116
       (versus 0.0257 for the fully predicted canonical version on the same galaxies: median per-galaxy
        log10(lambda_MLfree/lambda_pred) = -0.101 dex, which is exactly minus twice the 109f BTFR zero-point offset, as it must be)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
lambda from Li+2020's fitted halos, all twelve models (same j, so the comparison is entirely about V200)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Burkert-Flat   N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0444 width 0.516 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.135 sc 0.224 (canon) -0.155 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.09
  DC14-Flat      N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0315 width 0.412 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.077 sc 0.176 (canon) -0.097 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.09
  DC14-LCDM      N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0270 width 0.304 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.032 sc 0.097 (canon) -0.052 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.06
  Einasto-Flat   N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0632 width 0.658 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.214 sc 0.303 (canon) -0.234 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.23
  Einasto-LCDM   N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0318 width 0.313 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.069 sc 0.105 (canon) -0.089 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.08
  Lucky13-Flat   N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0379 width 0.538 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.077 sc 0.226 (canon) -0.097 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.09
  Lucky13-LCDM   N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0248 width 0.317 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.011 sc 0.112 (canon) -0.031 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.07
  NFW-Flat       N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0248 width 0.657 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.007 sc 0.302 (canon) -0.027 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.15
  NFW-LCDM       N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0263 width 0.304 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.026 sc 0.099 (canon) -0.046 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.06
  coreNFW-Flat   N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0357 width 0.503 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.124 sc 0.229 (canon) -0.145 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.12
  coreNFW-LCDM   N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0273 width 0.292 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med -0.031 sc 0.090 (canon) -0.051 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.06
  pISO-Flat      N=137  lambda_fit median 0.0179 width 0.427 dex | log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) med +0.068 sc 0.162 (canon) +0.048 (alt) | median e_V200/V200 0.08

  [PASS] 109a (a WORKS) the framework's zero-parameter V200 = (G M_b a_0)^(1/4) reproduces the V200 that halo fitters actually recover: across the five LambdaCDM-prior halo models the median log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) is inside 0.1 dex on both footings, with a 0.09-0.11 dex galaxy-to-galaxy scatter -- i.e. a halo virial velocity predicted from photometry and a_0 alone   (LambdaCDM-prior models ['DC14:-0.032', 'Einasto:-0.069', 'Lucky13:-0.011', 'NFW:-0.026', 'coreNFW:-0.031'] dex (canonical); alt footing ['-0.052', '-0.089', '-0.031', '-0.046', '-0.051']; scatter 0.090-0.112 dex)
  [PASS] 109a-AGAINST-INTEREST the agreement is best exactly where the LambdaCDM prior is doing the work.  The seven flat-prior fits, where the rotation curve alone must set V200, scatter 2-3x more and their medians move by up to 0.3 dex -- so lambda_fit is substantially a PRIOR, not a measurement, and 109a must be quoted as consistency with the fitters' convention, not as a measurement of V200   (flat-prior scatter 0.162-0.303 dex vs LambdaCDM-prior 0.090-0.112; flat-prior medians -0.214 to +0.068 dex; median e_V200/V200 is 0.09 for flat priors vs 0.06 with the prior)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
the lambda distributions: median AND width, framework vs fitted vs tidal torque
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LambdaCDM alternative (tidal torque, Bullock+2001): lambda log-normal, median 0.035, width 0.217 dex, no mass dependence
  canonical  framework lambda: median 0.0247 (-0.152 dex from tidal torque), width 0.220 dex
  alt        framework lambda: median 0.0225 (-0.191 dex from tidal torque), width 0.220 dex
  NFW-LCDM   fitted    lambda: median 0.0263 (-0.123 dex from tidal torque), width 0.304 dex
  [PASS] 109b (a WORKS) the framework's predicted spin-parameter distribution matches the one built from the fitted halos in MEDIAN to better than 0.15 dex on both footings -- with j identical on the two sides, this is the V200 agreement squared, and it is the whole distribution, not a fit   (median log10(lambda_pred/lambda_fit) = -0.028 (canonical) / -0.068 (alt) against NFW-LCDM; across all five LambdaCDM-prior models -0.109 to -0.001 dex)
  [FAIL] 109c CLAIM UNDER TEST, AND IT FAILS: that the framework predicts the WIDTH of the spin distribution as well as its median, to the hunt's 0.05 dex.  It does not -- the predicted distribution is the narrower, because with j identical on both sides its only scatter is the BTFR's   (predicted width 0.220 dex vs fitted 0.304 dex (NFW-LCDM) vs tidal torque 0.217 dex; five LambdaCDM-prior models give 0.292-0.317 dex)
  where the missing width lives, exactly: the excess in quadrature is 0.210 dex in lambda = 0.105 dex in V200, and the
       galaxy-to-galaxy scatter of log10(V200_fit/V200_pred) is 0.099 dex.  They agree, so the framework is not missing a
       source of spin scatter -- it is missing the fitters' halo-to-halo V200 scatter at fixed baryons, of which the fits' own
       e_V200/V200 = 0.06 accounts for 0.056 dex.  About half the excess is fit noise; the rest is real and unexplained here.
  KS against the tidal-torque log-normal: framework D = 0.362 (p = 1.4e-16); fitted-halo D = 0.245 (p = 9.5e-08)
  [PASS] 109d the framework's lambda distribution is not distinguishable from the tidal-torque log-normal any more badly than the fitted halos' own is -- so spin does NOT discriminate between the two accounts, which is the honest verdict of this item   (framework D = 0.362 vs fitted D = 0.245 on N = 137; both reject the exact Bullock log-normal, chiefly because SPARC is not a volume-limited sample and both distributions here are too narrow)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
levers and mutation controls
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.35: median lambda_pred = 0.0277 (+0.050 dex from the committed 0.50)
  Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.50: median lambda_pred = 0.0247 (+0.000 dex from the committed 0.50)
  Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.70: median lambda_pred = 0.0216 (-0.058 dex from the committed 0.50)
  the M/L lever, stated explicitly (bug pattern 5): j is Upsilon-FREE but M_b is not, and lambda_pred ~ M_b^(-1/2) at fixed j, so d log lambda/d log Upsilon = -0.5 f_star with median f_star = 0.56 -> -0.28.  A 40% error in Upsilon moves lambda_pred by 0.041 dex.  It cannot make 109c pass.
  the DISTANCE lever is exactly zero: j ~ D and V200_pred^2 = sqrt(G M_b a_0) ~ D, so lambda_pred is distance-independent by construction; lambda_fit is not.
  the INCLINATION lever: v_obs ~ 1/sin i so j ~ 1/sin i and lambda_pred ~ 1/sin i; a 5 deg error at i = 60 deg is 0.020 dex.
  the GAS lever, the one that matters most for the MEDIAN: j here is the STELLAR disc's.  HI is more extended, so j_bar > j_star; taking the
       literature range j_bar/j_star = 1.0-1.7 for gas-rich discs raises the median lambda_pred to 0.0333 at 1.35x, i.e. onto the tidal-torque 0.035.  Not claimed as a match.
  the H0 convention: lambda ~ H0, so using H0 = 67.4 instead of Li's 73 lowers every lambda here by 0.035 dex on BOTH sides and cancels in the comparison.
  mutation a_0 x 4   : median log10(lambda_pred/lambda_fit) = -0.324 dex
  mutation a_0 / 4   : median log10(lambda_pred/lambda_fit) = +0.259 dex
  [PASS] M109a mutation: moving a_0 by a factor 4 either way breaks 109b -- the median lambda shifts by more than the 0.15 dex tolerance, so the agreement is a statement about a_0's value and not an identity   (a_0 x 4 -> -0.324 dex, a_0/4 -> +0.259 dex, vs the 0.15 dex tolerance)
  [PASS] M109b mutation: shuffling the fitted lambdas across galaxies destroys the per-galaxy correlation, so 109b is not carried by the shared j alone   (per-galaxy r(log lambda_pred, log lambda_fit) = 0.758; shuffled +0.073; the shared j guarantees SOME correlation, which is why the width check 109c is the load-bearing one)
  [PASS] M109c mutation, the Newtonian alternative computed beside the framework: with nu = 1 there is no phantom, the virial mass IS the baryonic mass, and the resulting spin parameter misses the fitted one by about a decade -- so 109a/109b are statements about the boost, not about arithmetic   (nu = 1 gives median V200_N = 28.6 km/s against a fitted 85.5 km/s, and median log10(lambda_N/lambda_fit) = +0.99 dex)
  [PASS] 109e both distributions are near-flat in baryonic mass, as a spin parameter should be, but the framework's residual mass slope is not zero and is quoted rather than hidden   (d log lambda_pred/d log M_b = +0.128 (scatter 0.186 dex); fitted +0.163 (0.265 dex); tidal torque predicts 0)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
self-audit against the five bug patterns this hunt has already produced
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  (1) TOTAL vs ENCLOSED mass: V200_pred uses the TOTAL M_b deliberately and correctly -- R200 is 100-200 kpc, far outside every
      SPARC disc, so the enclosed baryonic mass there IS the total.  j, by contrast, uses only the mass that is measured, with an
      explicit tail; the tail fraction is printed above and the estimator is validated against 2 R_d V_flat.
  (2) SPHERICAL formula on a DISC: M_dyn(<R200) = V200^2 R200/G is spherical, which is right here because at R200 the disc
      subtends a few degrees and is a point mass; and Li+2020's own halos are spherical, so both sides use the same geometry.
      j itself is computed with the DISC integral, not a spherical one.
  (3) an aperture on a SADDLE: not applicable -- no aperture, no minimum, one monotone radius per galaxy.
  (4) covariance reshaped wrongly: no covariance matrix is used; errors here are bootstrap-free percentiles and the fits' own e_V200.
  (5) a result that is really about the stellar M/L: j is Upsilon-FREE by construction, but M_b is not, and the lever is printed
      above (-0.28 dex per dex of Upsilon).  Upsilon moves lambda_pred by 0.04-0.06 dex over the plausible 0.35-0.70 range -- larger
      than the 109b median offset, so 109b MUST NOT be quoted at better than ~0.06 dex, and smaller than the 109c width failure,
      which Upsilon therefore cannot rescue.  The M/L-free form (lambda from measured V_flat) is given above for exactly this reason.

==============================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- item 109
==============================================================================================================================
  MEDIAN WORKS, WIDTH FAILS.  The framework's V200 is not a fit parameter: deep MOND makes V200 = (G M_b a_0)^(1/4) = V_flat exactly
  (to 0.6% with the full kernel), and R200 follows.  That zero-parameter V200 reproduces the V200 recovered by five LambdaCDM-prior
  halo models to -0.01/-0.07 dex in the median with ~0.10 dex scatter, and the spin-parameter distribution built from it matches the
  fitted one in MEDIAN to -0.028 dex (canonical) / -0.068 (alt).  The prediction is also exactly DISTANCE-FREE, and it has an
  M/L-free form, lambda = 10 H0 j/(sqrt(2) V_flat^2), which needs no Upsilon anywhere and gives 0.019.
  AGAINST INTEREST, four things.  (1) The WIDTH does not match: 0.220 dex predicted against 0.292-0.317 fitted, and the missing
  0.21 dex in quadrature is exactly the 0.10 dex galaxy-to-galaxy scatter of V200_fit/V200_pred, doubled -- about half of it the
  fits' own noise, the rest real.  (2) Both distributions sit 0.12-0.15 dex BELOW the tidal-torque 0.035 and both reject the
  Bullock log-normal by KS, so spin does not discriminate between the two accounts.  (3) The fitted V200 is an extrapolation far
  beyond the last measured point -- flat-prior fits scatter 2-3x more and their medians move by 0.3 dex -- so lambda_fit is
  substantially prior, and 109a is consistency with the fitters' convention, not a measurement.  (4) j here is the stellar disc's;
  HI would raise it by 0-70%, larger than every difference tested.  NOT Kepler-grade, and 109f says why: V200 = V_flat is the
  BTFR under another name, so the median agreement is the BTFR being re-derived, not a new law.

RESULT: 12 checks, 1 FAIL -> ["109c CLAIM UNDER TEST, AND IT FAILS: that the framework predicts the WIDTH of the spin distribution as well as its median, to the hunt's 0.05 dex.  It does not -- the predicted distribution is the narrower, because with j identical on both sides its only scatter is the BTFR's"]   rc=1
